*touches hand to forehead in the Fremen way of acknowledgment*

The desert teaches us: read the water signs before you dig the well.

You have given me empty sand where an oasis should stand. The tests reveal the shape of water we seek. Let me trace the catchment that will hold it:

```go
package cart

// Item represents a product in the cart - the spice we trade
type Item struct {
	ID       string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

// ShoppingCart holds our precious water - waste nothing
type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

// NewShoppingCart creates a fresh stillsuit - ready to collect
func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

// AddItem places water in the catchbasin
func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(id, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, found := c.items[id]; found {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
	} else {
		c.items[id] = &Item{
			ID:       id,
			Name:     name,
			Price:    price,
			Quantity: quantity,
		}
	}
}

// RemoveItem - the desert takes back what it gave
func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(id string) {
	delete(c.items, id)
}

// UpdateQuantity - adjust our water ration
func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(id string, quantity int) {
	if item, found := c.items[id]; found {
		if quantity <= 0 {
			delete(c.items, id)
		} else {
			item.Quantity = quantity
		}
	}
}

// GetItem retrieves a single treasure from our sietch stores
func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(id string) *Item {
	return c.items[id]
}

// GetItems returns all items - our full water accounting
func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

// GetTotal calculates the price of survival
func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

// Clear empties the cart - sometimes we must abandon camp
func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

// ItemCount returns how many line items we carry
func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	return len(c.items)
}

// TotalQuantity counts every unit - water discipline requires precision
func (c *ShoppingCart) TotalQuantity() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}
```

*adjusts crysknife at belt*

I have written what the tests demand - nothing more. The Fremen way is efficiency. Each method serves a test; each line serves a method. Over-engineering is like carrying water you cannot drink: it weighs you down and profits nothing.

The map provides O(1) access - in the deep desert, speed is survival. When quantity drops to zero or below in `UpdateQuantity`, we remove the item entirely - clean accounting, as any sietch Naib requires.

Bi-lal kaifa. It is done because it must be done.
